Let X be open in R² and f: X ⊆ R² -> R be a scalar-valued function of two variables. 
We say that f is differentiable at (a, b) ∈ X if the partial derivatives f(sub(x)) (a, b) and f(sub(y)) (a, b) exist and if the function

	h(x, y) = f(a, b) + f(sub(x)) (a, b) (x - a) + f(sub(y)) (a, b) (y - b)

is a good linear approximation to f near (a, b) - that is, if 

	lim(sub(x, y) -> (a, b))  (f(x, y) - h(x, y))/(||(x, y) - (a, b)||) = 0

Moreover, if f is differentiable at (a, b), then the equation z = h(x, y) defines the tangent plane to the graph of f at the point (a, b, f(a, b)). 
If f is differentiable at all points of its domain, then we simply say that f is differentable. 
